text:
maria: Heads-up — onboarding team flagged a bunch of confusing SDK error surfaces in our sample apps. Users see `401`/`403`/`429` as raw HTTP text and the TypeScript SDK throws different shaped exceptions than Go. Can we agree on a minimal, cross-SDK error contract + retry hints?

ben: Definitely a problem. Current state: JS SDK throws `AuthError` with `code: 'AUTH'`, Go returns an `http` error with body string. No unified `code` stringenum.

sophie: This is causing real onboarding friction — saw a new user give up after 2 retries because sample app just logged "request failed". We should supply both machine-friendly fields and a short human message.

alejandro: Proposal: standard error envelope returned by the API + SDKs should map to typed exceptions. Minimal shape: `{ code, http_status, message, retryable?, retry_after? }`. Example: ```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED",
    "http_status": 429,
    "message": "quota exceeded for burst; try again",
    "retryable": true,
    "retry_after": 3
  }
}
```

maria: I like that. A few follow-ups: (1) do we standardize `code` values, (2) how do SDKs expose `retry_after` (seconds vs RFC header), (3) do we add an opt-in compatibility mode?

ben: For parity, SDKs should map the envelope to language-native typed exceptions: e.g., `new RateLimitError(message, {retryAfter: 3})` in TS and `type RateLimitError struct { Msg string; RetryAfter int }` in Go. Keep `code` as canonical string (`RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED`) so server logs and alerts can use it.

alejandro: On headers vs body: server should send both `Retry-After` header and the body field when applicable. SDKs should prefer body `retry_after` if present (more precise), otherwise fall back to header.

ben: Back-compat note: existing users might parse HTTP text. We should ship a 2-step rollout: 1) API starts including envelope; 2) SDKs add mapping behind a minor release flag; 3) Gradually turn on by default after customers have time.

sophie: Add a short sample snippet for onboarding that demonstrates handling and retrying gracefully. Minimal docs blurb + code sample will reduce support tickets.

maria: Agreed. Can we also make the human message concise and actionable (one-liner) and include a link to the troubleshooting KB for uncommon cases? e.g., "Invalid API key — check your env var: https://redwood.ai/docs/errors#auth"

ben: Draft sample (TS): ```ts
try {
  await client.generate(...);
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof RateLimitError && err.retryAfter) {
    await sleep(err.retryAfter * 1000);
    // retry once
  }
  throw err;
}
```

alejandro: CI note — sample-app PR has a lint fail when I tried to add the new error wrapper. ci-bot: any detail?

ci-bot: @alejandro CI pipeline (samples) failed: `samples/nextjs-app/src/utils/api.ts: missing export 'mapApiError'`. See: https://ci.redwood.internal/12345 

maria: I'll take the doc and sample action items. Ben/alejandro — can you sketch the SDK exception mapping RFC this week? Sophie — can you update onboarding checklist to include explicit error handling instructions?

ben: Yep. I'm going to draft an RFC and post in #devex by EOD. :thumbsup:

sophie: I'll patch the sample app and add a short e2e that asserts we surface `retry_after`.

alejandro: I'll fix the sample export and add unit tests for Go error mapping.

maria: Cool. Thanks all — this should make a big dent in onboarding churn. :raised_hands:
